.$  \n\nNote that the changes of variables\n\n$$\nu=y+2x,\\,v=y-2x\n$$\n\n \n\ngives us the *inverse* function ${T}^{-1},$ that is\n\n$$\n{T}^{-1}:\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}\\to \\begin{bmatrix}u(x,y) \\\\ v(x,y)\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nTo compute the required area, we proceed in four steps:\n\n**Step 1**: Find $Δ,$ which is the image of $D$ under the action of ${T}^{-1}.$ \n\nSubstituting $y+2x=u$ into the equation of the first line, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y+2x=1\\,⟹\\,u=1,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhile substituting $y-2x=v$ into the equation of the second line, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-2x=1\\,⟹\\,v=1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $u=y+2x$ and $v=y-2x$ into the equation of curve, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{2}-4{x}^{2}=4\\,⟹\\,(y+2x)(y-2x)=4,\\,⟹\\,uv=4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe intersection point of the two lines in the $uv$-plane is $(1,1).$\n\nTo find the intersection of the curve with the first line in the $uv$-plane, we solve the following system:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}uv &=4 \\\\ u &=1\\end{aligned}\\right.\\,⟹\\,\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}u &=1 \\\\ v &=4.\\end{aligned}\\right.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTo find the intersections of the curve with the second line in the $uv$-plane, we solve the following system:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}uv &=4 \\\\ v &=1\\end{aligned}\\right.\\,⟹\\,\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}u &=4 \\\\ v &=1.\\end{aligned}\\right.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nSo, the intersection points are $(1,1),$ $(1,4)$ and $(4,1).$\n\nTherefore, our domain in the $uv$-plane has the type I representation\n\n$$\nΔ=\\{(u,v)\\,:\\,1\\le u\\le 4,\\,1\\le v\\le \\frac{4}{u}\\}.\n$$\n\n![](images/30/30fff9564451baadff3cae48dbfb91dc08fc8591e596e7cd2f65d8cfc6c9c5a6.png)\n\n**Step 2**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & \\frac{∂u}{∂y} \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & \\frac{∂v}{∂y}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}2 & 1 \\\\ -2 & \\text{ }\\text{ }1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}={\\left(\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)}\\right)}^{-1}=(4)^{-1}=\\frac{1}{4}.\n$$\n\nNote that $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}\\ne 0$ everywhere inside $Δ.$  In other words, $T$ has no critical points inside $Δ.$\n\n**Step 4**: Compute the required area.\n\nWe calculate the required area by performing the change of variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}\\text{d}x\\text{d}y & ={∬}_{Δ}|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v \\\\  & ={∬}_{Δ}|\\frac{1}{4}|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}{\\int }_{1}^{4}[{\\int }_{1}^{4/u}\\,dv]du \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}{\\int }_{1}^{4}[v]_{1}^{4/u}\\,du \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}{\\int }_{1}^{4}(\\frac{4}{u}-1)du \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}{[4\\operatorname{ln}⁡u-u]}_{1}^{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}(4\\operatorname{ln}⁡4-4+1) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}(4\\operatorname{ln}⁡4-3) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡4-\\frac{3}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186371913 "The area of the required region is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}dxdy.\n$$\n\nLet's define a transformation $T$ as follows: \n\n$$\nT:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}\\to \\begin{bmatrix}x(u,v) \\\\ y(u,v)\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThis transformation maps some region $Δ$ in the $uv$-plane to our region $D$ in the $xy$-plane.\n\nTo compute the required area, we will use the change of variables formula\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}dxdy={∬}_{Δ}|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\text{ }dudv\n$$\n\nwhere $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}$ is the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T.$  \n\nNote that the changes of variables\n\n$$\nu=\\frac{1}{xy},\\,v={x}^{2}y\n$$\n\n \n\ngives us the *inverse* function ${T}^{-1},$ that is\n\n$$\n{T}^{-1}:\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}\\to \\begin{bmatrix}u(x,y) \\\\ v(x,y)\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nTo compute the required area, we proceed in four steps:\n\n**Step 1**: Find $Δ,$ which is the image of $D$ under the action of ${T}^{-1}.$ \n\nWe substitute $u=\\frac{1}{xy}$ into the equations of the first and second curves:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & y=\\frac{1}{2x} & ⟹\\, & \\frac{1}{xy}=2 & ⟹\\, & u=2 \\\\  & y=\\frac{1}{3x} & ⟹\\, & \\frac{1}{xy}=3 & ⟹\\, & u=3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAnd we substitute $v={x}^{2}y$ into the equations of the third and fourth curves:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & y=\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}} & ⟹\\, & {x}^{2}y=1 & ⟹\\, & v=1 \\\\  & y=\\frac{3}{{x}^{2}} & ⟹\\, & {x}^{2}y=3 & ⟹\\, & v=3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our domain in the $uv$-plane is the following rectangle:\n\n$$\nΔ=\\{(u,v)\\,:\\,2\\le u\\le 3,\\,1\\le v\\le 3\\}\n$$\n\n![](images/05/05d2ca47db245b147cb52f95a474a60ece144931c8b6e1e40c6628a99b53d690.png)\n\n**Step 2**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & \\frac{∂u}{∂y} \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & \\frac{∂v}{∂y}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}-\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}y} & -\\frac{1}{x{y}^{2}} \\\\ 2xy & \\text{ }\\text{ }{x}^{2}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{y}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}={\\left(\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)}\\right)}^{-1}=y=\\frac{{x}^{2}{y}^{2}}{{x}^{2}y}=\\frac{1}{{u}^{2}v}.\n$$\n\nNote that $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}\\ne 0$ everywhere inside $Δ.$  In other words, $T$ has no critical points inside $Δ.$\n\n**Step 4**: Compute the required area.\n\nWe calculate the required area by performing the change of variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}\\text{d}x\\text{d}y & ={∬}_{Δ}|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v \\\\  & ={∬}_{Δ}|-\\frac{1}{{u}^{2}v}|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v \\\\  & ={\\int }_{2}^{3}{\\int }_{1}^{3}\\frac{1}{{u}^{2}v}\\,\\text{d}u\\,\\text{d}v \\\\  & ={\\int }_{2}^{3}\\frac{1}{{u}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}u{\\int }_{1}^{3}\\frac{1}{v}\\,\\text{d}v \\\\  & =(-\\frac{1}{3}+\\frac{1}{2})\\cdot (\\operatorname{ln}⁡3-\\operatorname{ln}⁡1) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{6}\\operatorname{ln}⁡3.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186371974 "The area of the required region is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}dxdy.\n$$\n\nLet's define a transformation $T$ as follows: \n\n$$\nT:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}\\to \\begin{bmatrix}x(u,v) \\\\ y(u,v)\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nWe'll assume this transformation maps some region $Δ$ in the $uv$-plane to our region $D$ in the $xy$-plane.\n\nTo compute the required area, we will use the change of variables formula\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}dxdy={∬}_{Δ}|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\text{ }dudv,\n$$\n\n  \n\nwhere $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}$ is the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T.$\n\nTo compute the required area, we proceed in five steps:\n\n**Step 1**: Find a suitable change of variables.\n\nNotice that we can rewrite the equations of our curves as follows:\n\n$$\nxy=1,\\,xy=4,\\,\\frac{x}{{y}^{2}}=1,\\,\\frac{x}{{y}^{2}}=3\n$$\n\nThis suggests the following change of variables:\n\n$$\nu=xy,\\,v=\\frac{x}{{y}^{2}}\n$$\n\nThis change of variables gives us the *inverse* function ${T}^{-1},$ that is\n\n$$\n{T}^{-1}:\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}\\to \\begin{bmatrix}u(x,y) \\\\ v(x,y)\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Find $Δ,$ which is the image of $D$ under the action of ${T}^{-1}.$ \n\nWe substitute $u=xy$ into the equations of the first and second curves:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & xy=1\\,⟹\\,u=1 \\\\  & xy=4\\,⟹\\,u=4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAnd we substitute $v=\\frac{x}{{y}^{2}}$ into the equations of the third and fourth curves:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\frac{x}{{y}^{2}}=1\\,⟹\\,v=1 \\\\  & \\frac{x}{{y}^{2}}=3\\,⟹\\,v=3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our domain in the $uv$-plane is the following rectangle:\n\n$$\nΔ={(u,v)\\,:\\,1\\le u\\le 4,\\,1\\le v\\le 3}\n$$\n\n![](images/2d/2d98eb2de472226d010fb633478f3662754679c2256e3fd3d06600a6599bc7f5.png)\n\n**Step 3**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & \\frac{∂u}{∂y} \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & \\frac{∂v}{∂y}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}y & x \\\\ \\frac{1}{{y}^{2}} & -\\frac{2x}{{y}^{3}}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =-\\frac{2x}{{y}^{2}}-\\frac{x}{{y}^{2}} \\\\  & =-\\frac{3x}{{y}^{2}} \\\\  & =-3v.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 4**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}={\\left(\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)}\\right)}^{-1}=-\\frac{1}{3v}.\n$$\n\nNote that $\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)}\\ne 0$ everywhere inside $Δ.$  In other words, $T$ has no critical points inside $Δ.$\n\n**Step 5**: Compute the required area.\n\nWe calculate the required area by performing the change of variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}\\text{d}x\\text{d}y & ={∬}_{Δ}|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v \\\\  & ={∬}_{Δ}|-\\frac{1}{3v}|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}{∬}_{Δ}\\frac{1}{v}\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}{\\int }_{1}^{4}\\,\\text{d}u{\\int }_{1}^{3}\\frac{1}{v}\\,\\text{d}v \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot (4-1)\\cdot (\\operatorname{ln}⁡3-\\operatorname{ln}⁡1) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡3\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186372020 "Let $S$ be an oriented piecewise-smooth surface bounded by a simple, closed, piecewise-smooth boundary $C$ with positive orientation. If a vector field $F$ has continuous partial derivatives for all its components in an open region in space that contains $S,$ then Stokes' theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot dr={∬}_{S}\\text{curl}\\,F\\cdot \\text{d}S.\n$$\n\nThere are many surfaces whose boundary is the curve $C$ representing the intersection of the half-cone $z=-\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}$ and the plane $z=-3.$ One such surface would be the plane $S$ with the equation \n\n$$\nz=g(x,y)=-3.\n$$\n\nWe first compute the partial derivatives of the graph $z=g(x,y):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂g}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(-3)=0 \\\\ \\frac{∂g}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(-3)=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute the equation $z=g(x,y)$ into the expression for $\\text{curl}\\,F:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{curl}\\,F\\,{|}_{z=g(x,y)} & =⟨y,\\,2yz,\\,-{z}^{2}+1⟩{|}_{z=-3} \\\\  & =⟨\\underbrace{y}_{P},\\,\\underbrace{-6y}_{Q},\\,\\underbrace{-8}_{R}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}\\text{curl}\\,F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}(-P\\frac{∂g}{∂x}-Q\\frac{∂g}{∂y}+R)\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(-y\\cdot 0-(-6y)\\cdot 0-8)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}-8\\,\\text{d}A,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $D$ is region enclosed by the projection of $C$ onto the $xy$-plane.\n\nNotice that the projection of $C$ onto the $xy$-plane is the circle ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=9$ with radius $3$ centered at the origin. Therefore, using the change of variables to polar coordinates\n\n$$\nx=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,\\text{d}A=r\\,\\text{d}r\\text{d}\\theta ,\n$$\n\n \n\nwe obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}-8\\,\\text{d}A & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{3}-8\\cdot r\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =-8{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\,\\text{d}\\theta \\cdot {\\int }_{0}^{3}r\\,\\text{d}r \\\\  & =-8[\\theta ]_{\\theta =0}^{\\theta =2\\pi }\\cdot {[\\frac{{r}^{2}}{2}]}_{r=0}^{r=3} \\\\  & =-8(2\\pi -0)\\cdot (\\frac{9}{2}-0) \\\\  & =-72\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186372027 "Let $S$ be an oriented piecewise-smooth surface bounded by a simple, closed, piecewise-smooth boundary $C$ with positive orientation. If a vector field $F$ has continuous partial derivatives for all its components in an open region in space that contains $S,$ then Stokes' theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot dr={∬}_{S}\\text{curl}\\,F\\cdot \\text{d}S.\n$$\n\nThere are many surfaces whose boundary is the curve $C$ representing the intersection of the cylinder ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=1$ and the plane $z=2+2x-y.$ One such surface would be the plane $S$ with the equation \n\n$$\nz=g(x,y)=2+2x-y.\n$$\n\nWe first compute the partial derivatives of the graph $z=g(x,y):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂g}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(2+2x-y)=2 \\\\ \\frac{∂g}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(2+2x-y)=-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute the equation $z=g(x,y)$ into the expression for $\\text{curl}\\,F:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{curl}\\,F\\,{|}_{z=g(x,y)} & =⟨-y,\\,z,\\,-2x⟩{|}_{z=2+2x-y} \\\\  & =⟨\\underbrace{-y}_{P},\\,\\underbrace{2+2x-y}_{Q},\\,\\underbrace{-2x}_{R}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}\\text{curl}\\,F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}(-P\\frac{∂g}{∂x}-Q\\frac{∂g}{∂y}+R)\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(-(-y)\\cdot (2)-(2+2x-y)\\cdot (-1)-2x)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(2y+2+2x-y-2x)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(2+y)\\,\\text{d}A,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $D$ is region enclosed by the projection of $C$ onto the $xy$-plane.\n\nNotice that the projection of $C$ onto the $xy$-plane is the circle ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=1$ with radius $1$ centered at the origin. Therefore, using the change of variables to polar coordinates\n\n$$\nx=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,\\text{d}A=r\\,\\text{d}r\\text{d}\\theta ,\n$$\n\n \n\nwe obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}(2+y)\\,\\text{d}A & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{1}(2+r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\cdot r\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{1}2r+{r}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{[{r}^{2}+\\frac{{r}^{3}}{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ]}_{0}^{1}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }(1+\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={[\\theta -\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ]}_{0}^{2\\pi } \\\\  & =2\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186372034 "A function $ψ(x,y)$ is a stream function of the vector field $F=P\\,i+Q\\,j$ if the following equations hold:\n\n$$\nP=\\frac{∂ψ}{∂y},\\,Q=-\\frac{∂ψ}{∂x}\n$$\n\nComputing the partial derivatives of the function $ψ(x,y),$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P & =\\frac{∂ψ}{∂y} \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{3}-3x{y}^{2}) \\\\  & =-6xy, \\\\ Q & =-\\frac{∂ψ}{∂x} \\\\  & =-\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{3}-3x{y}^{2}) \\\\  & =-3{x}^{2}+3{y}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the vector field with the stream function $ψ(x,y)={x}^{3}-3x{y}^{2}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(x,y) & =P\\,i+Q\\,j \\\\  & =-6xy\\,i-(3{x}^{2}-3{y}^{2})\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186372041 "A function $ψ(x,y)$ is a stream function of the vector field $F=P\\,i+Q\\,j$ if the following equations hold:\n\n$$\nP=\\frac{∂ψ}{∂y},\\,Q=-\\frac{∂ψ}{∂x}\n$$\n\nComputing the partial derivatives of the function $ψ(x,y),$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P & =\\frac{∂ψ}{∂y} \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}({y}^{2}-{x}^{2}+x) \\\\  & =2y, \\\\ Q & =-\\frac{∂ψ}{∂x} \\\\  & =-\\frac{∂}{∂x}({y}^{2}-{x}^{2}+x) \\\\  & =2x-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the vector field with the stream function $ψ(x,y)={y}^{2}-{x}^{2}+x$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(x,y) & =P\\,i+Q\\,j \\\\  & =2y\\,i+(2x-1)\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186372048 "A function $ψ(x,y)$ is a stream function of the vector field $F=P\\,i+Q\\,j$ if the following equations hold:\n\n$$\nP=\\frac{∂ψ}{∂y},\\,Q=-\\frac{∂ψ}{∂x}\n$$\n\nComputing the partial derivatives of the function $ψ(x,y),$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P & =\\frac{∂ψ}{∂y} \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}({e}^{x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡y-x) \\\\  & =-{e}^{x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡y, \\\\ Q & =-\\frac{∂ψ}{∂x} \\\\  & =-\\frac{∂}{∂x}({e}^{x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡y-x) \\\\  & =-{e}^{x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡y+1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the vector field with the stream function $ψ(x,y)={e}^{x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡y-x$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(x,y) & =P\\,i+Q\\,j \\\\  & =-{e}^{x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡y\\,i+(-{e}^{x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡y+1)\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186372062 "A vector field $F=P\\,i+Q\\,j$ with a simply connected domain $D⊆{R}^{2}$ has a stream function $ψ(x,y)$ if and only if\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y}=0.\n$$\n\nNote that $F(x,y)={e}^{x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡y\\,i-{e}^{x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡y\\,j$ is a vector field with simply connected domain $D={R}^{2},$ where $P(x,y)={e}^{x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡y$ and $Q(x,y)=-{e}^{x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡y.$ Computing the partial derivatives, we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂x}={e}^{x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡y,\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂y} & =-{e}^{x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, $F$ has a stream function, since\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y}={e}^{x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡y+(-{e}^{x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡y)=0.\n$$\n\nNext, we find a stream function. A function $ψ(x,y)$ is a stream function of the vector field $F=P\\,i+Q\\,j$ if \n\n$$\nP=\\frac{∂ψ}{∂y},\\,Q=-\\frac{∂ψ}{∂x}.\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂ψ}{∂y}={e}^{x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡y,\\,\\frac{∂ψ}{∂x}={e}^{x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡y.\n$$\n\nIntegrating these equations, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\nψ(x,y)={e}^{x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡y+f(x),\\,ψ(x,y)={e}^{x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡y+g(y)\n$$\n\nwhere $f$ and $g$ are arbitrary functions. Finally, setting $f=g=0,$ we have\n\n$$\nψ(x,y)={e}^{x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡y.\n$$"] [17592186372069 "A vector field $F=P\\,i+Q\\,j$ with a simply connected domain $D⊆{R}^{2}$ has a stream function $ψ(x,y)$ if and only if\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y}=0.\n$$\n\nNote that $F(x,y)=3({x}^{2}-{y}^{2})\\,i-6xy\\,j$ is a vector field with simply connected domain $D={R}^{2},$ where $P(x,y)=3({x}^{2}-{y}^{2})$ and $Q(x,y)=-6xy.$ Computing the partial derivatives, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂x}=6x,\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂y} & =-6x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, $F$ has a stream function, since\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y}=6x+(-6x)=0.\n$$\n\nNext, we find a stream function. A function $ψ(x,y)$ is a stream function of the vector field $F=P\\,i+Q\\,j$ if \n\n$$\nP=\\frac{∂ψ}{∂y},\\,Q=-\\frac{∂ψ}{∂x}.\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂ψ}{∂y}=3({x}^{2}-{y}^{2}),\\,\\frac{∂ψ}{∂x}=6xy.\n$$\n\nIntegrating these equations, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\nψ(x,y)=3{x}^{2}y-{y}^{3}+f(x),\\,ψ(x,y)=3{x}^{2}y+g(y)\n$$\n\nwhere $f$ and $g$ are arbitrary functions. Finally, setting $f=0,$ and $g(y)=-{y}^{3},$ we have\n\n$$\nψ(x,y)=3{x}^{2}y-{y}^{3}.\n$$"] [17592186372076 "A vector field $F=P\\,i+Q\\,j$ with a simply connected domain $D⊆{R}^{2}$ has a stream function $ψ(x,y)$ if and only if\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y}=0.\n$$\n\nNote that $F(x,y)=x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xy)\\,i-x\\operatorname{sin}⁡(xy)\\,j$ is a vector field with simply connected domain $D={R}^{2},$ where $P(x,y)=x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xy)$ and $Q(x,y)=-x\\operatorname{sin}⁡(xy).$ Computing the partial derivatives, we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂x}=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xy)-xy\\operatorname{sin}⁡(xy),\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂y} & =-{x}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xy).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, $F$ has a no stream function, since \n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y}=(1-{x}^{2})\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xy)-xy\\operatorname{sin}⁡(xy)\\ne 0.\n$$"] [17592186372083 "A vector field $F=P\\,i+Q\\,j$ with a simply connected domain $D⊆{R}^{2}$ has a stream function $ψ(x,y)$ if and only if\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y}=0.\n$$\n\nNote that $F(x,y)=2xy\\,i+({y}^{2}-{x}^{2})\\,j$ is a vector field with simply connected domain $D={R}^{2},$ where $P(x,y)=2xy$ and $Q(x,y)={y}^{2}-{x}^{2}.$ Computing the partial derivatives, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂x}=2y,\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂y} & =2y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, $F$ has a no stream function, since\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y}=2y+2y=4y\\ne 0.\n$$"] [17592186372146 "The streamlines of a vector field are the level curves of its stream function and are tangential to the vector field at every point. \n\nIf $F$ represents the velocity field of a fluid flow, then they show the path traced out by a massless particle as it moves with the flow.\n\nOf the given options, the only level curves tangential to the vector field at every point are shown below.\n\n![](images/b1/b1f19c7433608e58c3284f64eb6fc04622699d1539bc34899c0c470e4a89f6dc.png)"] [17592186372153 "A vector field $F=P\\,i+Q\\,j$ with a simply connected domain $D⊆{R}^{2}$ has a stream function $ψ(x,y)$ if and only if\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y}=0.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each vector field.\n\n- Vector field $F(x,y)=3x\\,i-{y}^{2}\\,j$ doesn't have a stream function. The partial derivatives are given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂x}=3,\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂y} & =-2y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y}=3+(-2y)\\ne 0.\n$$\n\n- Vector field $F(x,y)=x\\,i+x{y}^{2}\\,j$ doesn't have a stream function. The partial derivatives are given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂x}=1,\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂y} & =2xy.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y}=1+2xy\\ne 0.\n$$\n\n- Vector field $F(x,y)={x}^{2}\\,i-2xy\\,j$ has a stream function. Indeed, the partial derivatives are given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂x}=2x,\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂y} & =-2x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y}=2x+(-2x)=0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is $F(x,y)={x}^{2}\\,i-2xy\\,j$ only."] [17592186372160 "A vector field $F=P\\,i+Q\\,j$ with a simply connected domain $D⊆{R}^{2}$ has a stream function $ψ(x,y)$ if and only if\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y}=0.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each vector field.\n\n- Vector field $F(x,y)=4{y}^{2}x\\,i-x{y}^{2}\\,j$ doesn't have a stream function. The partial derivatives are given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂x}=4{y}^{2},\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂y} & =-2xy.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y}=4{y}^{2}+(-2xy)\\ne 0.\n$$\n\n- Vector field $F(x,y)={e}^{x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡y\\,i+{e}^{x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡y\\,j$ has a stream function. Indeed, the partial derivatives are given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂x}={e}^{x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡y,\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂y} & =-{e}^{x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y}={e}^{x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡y+(-{e}^{x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡y)=0.\n$$\n\n- Vector field $F(x,y)=2yx\\,i-{y}^{2}\\,j$ has a stream function. Indeed, the partial derivatives are given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂x}=2y,\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂y} & =-2y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y}=2y+(-2y)=0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is $F(x,y)={e}^{x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡y\\,i+{e}^{x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡y\\,j$; $F(x,y)=2yx\\,i-{y}^{2}\\,j$ only."] [17592186372167 "A vector field $F=P\\,i+Q\\,j$ with a simply connected domain $D⊆{R}^{2}$ has a stream function $ψ(x,y)$ if and only if\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y}=0.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each vector field.\n\n- Vector field $F(x,y)=(y+x)\\,i-y\\,j$ has a stream function. Indeed, the partial derivatives are given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂x}=1,\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂y} & =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y}=1+(-1)=0\n$$\n\n- Vector field $F(x,y)=(2x+2y)\\,i+2xy\\,j$ doesn't have a stream function. The partial derivatives are given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂x}=2,\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂y} & =2x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y}=2+2y\\ne 0.\n$$\n\n- Vector field $F(x,y)={x}^{2}{y}^{3}\\,i+4y\\,j$ doesn't have a stream function. The partial derivatives are given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂x}=2x{y}^{3},\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂y} & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y}=2x{y}^{3}+4\\ne 0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is $F(x,y)=(y+x)\\,i-y\\,j$ only."] [17592186372174 "A vector field $F=P\\,i+Q\\,j$ with a simply connected domain $D⊆{R}^{2}$ has a stream function $ψ(x,y)$ if and only if\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y}=0.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each vector field.\n\n- Vector field $F(x,y)=y{e}^{x}\\,i+x{e}^{y}\\,j$ doesn't have a stream function. The partial derivatives are given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂x}=y{e}^{x},\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂y} & =x{e}^{y}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y}=y{e}^{x}+x{e}^{y}\\ne 0.\n$$\n\n- Vector field $F(x,y)$ $=\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\operatorname{sin}⁡y\\,i$ $-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡y\\,j$ has a stream function. The partial derivatives are given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂x}=-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{sin}⁡y,\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂y} & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{sin}⁡y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y}=\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{sin}⁡y+(-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{sin}⁡y)=0.\n$$\n\n- Vector field $F(x,y)={x}^{2}y\\,i-2y\\,j$ doesn't have a stream function. The partial derivatives are given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂x}=2xy,\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂y} & =-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y}=2xy+(-2)\\ne 0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is $F(x,y)$ $=\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\operatorname{sin}⁡y\\,i$ $-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡y\\,j$ only."] [17592186372181 "The streamlines of a vector field are the level curves of its stream function and are tangential to the vector field at every point. \n\nIf $F$ represents the velocity field of a fluid flow, then they show the path traced out by a massless particle as it moves with the flow.\n. \n\nA vector field $F=P\\,i+Q\\,j$ with a simply connected domain $D⊆{R}^{2}$ has a stream function $ψ(x,y)$ if and only if\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y}=0.\n$$\n\nNote that $F(x,y)={e}^{x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡y\\,i-{e}^{x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡y\\,j$ is a vector field with simply connected domain $D={R}^{2},$ where $P(x,y)={e}^{x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡y$ and $Q(x,y)=-{e}^{x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡y.$ Computing the partial derivatives, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂x}={e}^{x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡y,\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂y} & =-{e}^{x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, $F$ has a stream function, since\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y}={e}^{x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡y+(-{e}^{x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡y)=0.\n$$\n\nNext, we find a stream function. A function $ψ(x,y)$ is a stream function of the vector field $F=P\\,i+Q\\,j$ if \n\n$$\nP=\\frac{∂ψ}{∂y},\\,Q=-\\frac{∂ψ}{∂x}.\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂ψ}{∂y}={e}^{x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡y,\\,\\frac{∂ψ}{∂x}={e}^{x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡y.\n$$\n\nIntegrating these equations, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\nψ(x,y)={e}^{x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡y+f(x),\\,ψ(x,y)={e}^{x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡y+g(y)\n$$\n\nwhere $f$ and $g$ are arbitrary functions. Finally, setting $f=g=0,$ we have\n\n$$\nψ(x,y)={e}^{x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡y.\n$$\n\nFinally, we find the level curve of the stream function $ψ(x,y)={e}^{x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡y$ that passes through $P.$\nFirst, we find the value of the stream function at $P:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}ψ(0,\\pi ) & ={e}^{(0)}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi ) \\\\  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, the streamline that passes through $P(0,\\pi )$ is the $0$-level curve:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}ψ(x,y) & =0 \\\\ {e}^{x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡y & =0 \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡y & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our possible streamlines are $y=k\\pi $ for any integer $k.$ The streamline that passes through $P(0,\\pi )$ is $y=\\pi .$\nThe vector field and the streamline through the point $P$ are shown below.\n\n![](images/bd/bd732367a876d2e6bba6bf96728ebcf8430176d2f480bff30a054cac5389b1f2.png)"] [17592186372209 "Let $S$ be an oriented piecewise-smooth surface bounded by a simple, closed, piecewise-smooth boundary $C$ with positive orientation. If a vector field $F$ has continuous partial derivatives for all its components in an open region in space that contains $S,$ then Stokes' theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot dr={∬}_{S}\\text{curl}\\,F\\cdot \\text{d}S.\n$$\n\nAlong the curve $C,$ we have\n\n$$\nx=\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,y=\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\\,z=1.\n$$\n\nSo,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(r(t)) & =z\\,i+3x\\,j+2{y}^{2}\\,k \\\\  & =\\,i+3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,j+2{sin}^{2}⁡t\\,k.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing ${r}^{′}(t),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)\\,j+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(1)\\,k \\\\  & =-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,i+\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,j+0\\,k.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t) & =(\\,i+3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,j+2{sin}^{2}⁡t\\,k)\\cdot (-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,i+\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,j+0\\,k) \\\\  & =1\\cdot (-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)+3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡t+2{sin}^{2}⁡t\\cdot 0 \\\\  & =-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t+3{cos}^{2}⁡t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate the integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}\\text{curl}\\,F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∮}_{C}F\\cdot dr \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }F(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }(-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t+3{cos}^{2}⁡t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }(-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t+\\frac{3}{2}(1+\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t))\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }(-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t+\\frac{3}{2}+\\frac{3}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={[\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+\\frac{3}{2}t+\\frac{3}{4}\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t]}_{0}^{2\\pi } \\\\  & =(1+\\frac{3}{2}(2\\pi )+0)-(1+\\frac{3}{2}(0)+0) \\\\  & =3\\pi \\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186372216 "Let $S$ be an oriented piecewise-smooth surface bounded by a simple, closed, piecewise-smooth boundary $C$ with positive orientation. If a vector field $F$ has continuous partial derivatives for all its components in an open region in space that contains $S,$ then Stokes' theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot dr={∬}_{S}\\text{curl}\\,F\\cdot \\text{d}S.\n$$\n\nAlong the curve $C,$ we have\n\n$$\nx=\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,y=\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\\,z=1-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t.\n$$\n\nSo,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(r(t)) & =(y-z)\\,i+(z-x)\\,j+(x-y)\\,k \\\\  & =(\\operatorname{sin}⁡t-(1-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t))\\,i+(1-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)\\,j+(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)\\,k \\\\  & =(2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t-1)\\,i+(1-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)\\,j+(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)\\,k.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing ${r}^{′}(t),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)\\,j+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(1-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)\\,k \\\\  & =-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,i+\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,j-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,k.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t) & =((2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t-1)\\,i+(1-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)\\,j+(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)\\,k)\\cdot (-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,i+\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,j-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,k) \\\\  & =(2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t-1)\\cdot (-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)+(1-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡t+(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)\\cdot (-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t) \\\\  & =-2{sin}^{2}⁡t+\\operatorname{sin}⁡t+\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-{cos}^{2}⁡t-{cos}^{2}⁡t+\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡t+\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-2{sin}^{2}⁡t-2{cos}^{2}⁡t \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡t+\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate the integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}\\text{curl}\\,F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∮}_{C}F\\cdot dr \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }F(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }(\\operatorname{sin}⁡t+\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-2)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =[-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+\\operatorname{sin}⁡t-2t]_{0}^{2\\pi } \\\\  & =(-1+0-2(2\\pi ))-(-1+0-2(0)) \\\\  & =-4\\pi \\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186373006 "First, we bring all variables to one side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2x+y) \\\\ 0 & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2x+y)-z\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nSo, if we set $f(x,y,z)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2x+y)-z$, then our surface can be viewed as the level surface $f(x,y,z)=0.$ \n\nA normal vector to the level surface that passes through the point $(1,-1,0)$ can be found by computing $∇f(1,-1,0).$\n\nSo, we find the gradient and evaluate it at $(1,-1,0):$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y,z) & ={f}_{x}(x,y,z)\\,i+{f}_{y}(x,y,z)\\text{ }j+{f}_{z}(x,y,z)\\text{ }k \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2x+y)-z)i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2x+y)-z)j+\\frac{∂}{∂z}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2x+y)-z)k \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{2x+y}\\text{ }i+\\frac{1}{2x+y}\\text{ }j-1\\text{ }k \\\\ ∇f(1,-1,0) & =\\frac{2}{2(1)+(-1)}\\text{ }i+\\frac{1}{2(1)+(-1)}\\text{ }j-k \\\\  & =2\\text{ }i+\\,j-k \\\\  & =⟨2,\\,1,\\,-1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, by using the symmetric form of the equation of the line that is parallel to the vector $v=⟨{v}_{x},{v}_{y},{v}_{z}⟩$ and passes through the point $({x}_{0},{y}_{0},{z}_{0})$,  we get: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{x-{x}_{0}}{{v}_{x}}=\\frac{y-{y}_{0}}{{v}_{y}}=\\frac{z-{z}_{0}}{{v}_{z}} \\\\ \\frac{x-1}{2}=\\frac{y-(-1)}{1}=\\frac{z-0}{-1} \\\\ \\frac{x-1}{2}=y+1=-z\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186373125 "If we set $$\n\\displaystyle f(x,y)=\\frac{{y}^{2}}{16}-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{9},\n$$ our curve can be viewed as the level curve $f(x,y)=1.$  \n\nA normal vector to the level curve that passes through the point $(0,4)$ can be found by computing $∇f(0,4).$ \n\nSo, we find the gradient and evaluate it at $(0,4):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & ={f}_{x}(x,y)\\,i+{f}_{y}(x,y)\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\frac{{y}^{2}}{16}-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{9})\\,i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\frac{{y}^{2}}{16}-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{9})\\,j \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{9}x\\,i+\\frac{1}{8}y\\,j \\\\ ∇f(0,4) & =-\\frac{2}{9}(0)\\,i+\\frac{1}{8}(4)\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\,j \\\\  & =⟨0,\\frac{1}{2}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, $∇f(0,4)=⟨0,\\frac{1}{2}⟩$ is a normal vector to the curve at $(0,4).$\n\nFinally, the Cartesian equation of the line that is parallel to $v=⟨{v}_{x},{v}_{y}⟩$ and passes through the point $({x}_{0},{y}_{0})$ can be written as:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x-{x}_{0}){v}_{y}-(y-{y}_{0}){v}_{x} & =0 \\\\ (x-0)\\frac{1}{2}-(y-4)0 & =0 \\\\ \\frac{1}{2}x & =0 \\\\ x & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186373139 "If we set $f(x,y,z)={x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}$, our surface can be viewed as the level surface $f(x,y,z)=3.$ \n\nA normal vector to the level surface that passes through the point $(-1,-1,1)$ can be found by computing $∇f(-1,-1,1).$ \n\nSo, we find the gradient and evaluate it at $(-1,-1,1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y,z) & ={f}_{x}(x,y,z)\\,i+{f}_{y}(x,y,z)\\text{ }j+{f}_{z}(x,y,z)\\text{ }k \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2})i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2})j+\\frac{∂}{∂z}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2})k \\\\  & =2x\\text{ }i+2y\\text{ }j+2z\\text{ }k \\\\ ∇f(-1,-1,1) & =2(-1)\\text{ }i+2(-1)\\text{ }j+2(1)\\text{ }k \\\\  & =-2\\,i-2\\,j+2\\,k \\\\  & =⟨-2,-2,2⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the Cartesian equation of the line that is parallel to $v=⟨{v}_{x},{v}_{y},{v}_{z}⟩$ and passes through the point $({x}_{0},{y}_{0},{z}_{0})$ can be written as:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{x-{x}_{0}}{{v}_{x}}=\\frac{y-{y}_{0}}{{v}_{y}}=\\frac{z-{z}_{0}}{{v}_{z}} \\\\ \\frac{x-(-1)}{-2}=\\frac{y-(-1)}{-2}=\\frac{z-1}{2} \\\\ \\frac{x+1}{-2}=\\frac{y+1}{-2}=\\frac{z-1}{2} \\\\ \\frac{x+1}{1}=\\frac{y+1}{1}=\\frac{z-1}{-1} \\\\ x+1=y+1=\\frac{z-1}{-1}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186373311 "The area of the required region is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}dxdy.\n$$\n\nLet's define a transformation $T$ as follows: \n\n$$\nT:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}\\to \\begin{bmatrix}x(u,v) \\\\ y(u,v)\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThis transformation maps some region $Δ$ in the $uv$-plane to our region $D$ in the $xy$-plane.\n\nTo compute the required area, we will use the change of variables formula\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}dxdy={∬}_{Δ}|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\text{ }dudv\n$$\n\nwhere $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}$ is the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T.$  \n\nNote that the changes of variables\n\n$$\nu=x-y,\\,v=y+1\n$$\n\n \n\ngives us the *inverse* function ${T}^{-1},$ that is\n\n$$\n{T}^{-1}:\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}\\to \\begin{bmatrix}u(x,y) \\\\ v(x,y)\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nTo compute the required area, we proceed in four steps:\n\n**Step 1**: Find $Δ,$ which is the image of $D$ under the action of ${T}^{-1}.$ \n\nSubstituting $x-y=u$ and $y=v+1$ into the equation of the first curve, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x-y)^{2}+y=2\\,⟹\\,(y+1)=3-(x-y)^{2}\\,⟹\\,v=3-{u}^{2},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nwhile substituting $y=v-1$ into the equation of the line, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y=0\\,⟹\\,v=1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the intersections of the curves in the $uv$-plane, we solve the following system:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}v &=3-{u}^{2} \\\\ v &=1\\end{aligned}\\right.\\,⟹\\,\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}u &=\\pm \\sqrt{2} \\\\ v &=1.\\end{aligned}\\right.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the intersection points are $(\\pm \\sqrt{2},1).$\n\nTherefore, our domain in the $uv$-plane has the type I representation\n\n$$\nΔ={(u,v)\\,:\\,-\\sqrt{2}\\le u\\le \\sqrt{2},\\,1\\le v\\le 3-{u}^{2}.}.\n$$\n\n![](images/9c/9cec7ff29557aa14cd19309e9180482a44091e7ef215205285822674e7bc25b0.png)\n\n \n\n**Step 2**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & \\frac{∂u}{∂y} \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & \\frac{∂v}{∂y}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}={\\left(\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)}\\right)}^{-1}=(1)^{-1}=1.\n$$\n\nNote that $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}\\ne 0$ everywhere inside $Δ.$  In other words, $T$ has no critical points inside $Δ.$\n\n**Step 4**: Compute the required area.\n\nWe calculate the required area by performing the change of variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}\\text{d}x\\text{d}y & ={∬}_{Δ}|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v \\\\  & ={∬}_{Δ}\\text{d}u\\text{d}v \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-\\sqrt{2}}^{\\sqrt{2}}[{\\int }_{1}^{3-{u}^{2}}\\,dv]du \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-\\sqrt{2}}^{\\sqrt{2}}[v]_{1}^{3-{u}^{2}}\\,du \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-\\sqrt{2}}^{\\sqrt{2}}(3-{u}^{2}-1)\\,du \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-\\sqrt{2}}^{\\sqrt{2}}(2-{u}^{2})\\,du \\\\  & ={[2u-\\frac{{u}^{3}}{3}]}_{-\\sqrt{2}}^{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\  & =(2\\sqrt{2}-\\frac{2\\sqrt{2}}{3})-(-2\\sqrt{2}+\\frac{2\\sqrt{2}}{3}) \\\\  & =\\frac{8\\sqrt{2}}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$"]}
